  "body": "The docs were current as of **5.41.1**. Eight releases landed since. Refreshing them turned up three things that were wrong *before* this arc started, which are worth more than the refresh itself.\n\n## Already wrong\n\n**`ARCHITECTURE.md` had been describing deleted code as current architecture since 5.44.0.** It listed *quota probes*, *command prefix detection* and *filepath extraction* by name, with their behaviour, as three of five live optimization rules. All three were removed three releases ago after firing **zero** times in 153,198 real requests.\n\nThe contract test written for that deletion pins `Settings`, the admin manifest, the handlers, the detectors and `.env.example` — so a partial revert fails — and says **nothing whatsoever about prose**. It passed the entire time.\n\n**The RTK pinned version was stale in three separate places** after one bump — still `0.44.2` in the README feature table, `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `USAGE.md`.\n\n**`FALLBACK_SKIP_KINDS` had never been documented in any user-facing doc, in any release.** Not stale — absent. Nothing looks for absence.\n\n## Repo identity, on the owner's call\n\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` told contributors to clone `Alishahryar1/free-claude-code`, and `RELEASE-CHECKLIST.md` called that the *\"primary fork / development remote\"*. Neither matches how the repo is run: all development and every release goes to `FiredMosquito831/my-claude-code`, and upstream is never pushed to. A contributor following the old instructions cloned a repo containing none of the work and built a package whose name didn't match their checkout.\n\nBoth now describe the real topology, with upstream kept for provenance and the `gh --repo` trap called out.\n\n## Added for the arc\n\n- **README** — Token Optimizer feature row and section, panel by panel against the real UI labels; a *Requests Answered Locally* subsection covering the two new log fields and the `answered locally · <rule>` display (which is also a **working filter value**); the `context_length` paragraph in failover.\n- **USAGE** — the Token Optimizer page and its trimming subsection nested *under* the existing Analytics section, so **no anchor was renamed**; `context_length`; RTK `gain`, pin drift, and an explicit statement that MCC forces RTK's telemetry opt-out and suppresses its consent prompt, so enabling RTK through MCC cannot opt anyone in.\n- **ARCHITECTURE** — the corrected two-rule list with why the others went; the new failure kind; the `tool_result_trimming` module and its core/config boundary.\n\n**Tool-result trimming is documented as off and probably best left off**, with the measured numbers rather than a caution: 10.9% more fresh input tokens at the shipped defaults, one near-total cache miss when enabled mid-conversation, break-even at a ~90.9% baseline hit rate. Numbers read from the module docstring and `.env.example`, **not retyped from the brief.**\n\n## Counts checked rather than assumed\n\n| claim | verified against | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| five optimizations | `OPTIMIZATION_RULES` | **wrong → two** |\n| RTK v0.44.2 ×3 | `config/rtk.py: RTK_VERSION` | **wrong → 0.45.0** |\n| 56 providers ×3 | `len(PROVIDER_CATALOG)` | correct |\n| 14 search providers | `len(WEBSEARCH_CATALOG)` | correct |\n| 66 advanced options ×2 | summed `advanced_options` | correct |\n| nine desktop settings ×2 | 9 `desktop_*` fields | correct |\n\nOne *suspected*-stale claim — the README's \"capped at 50k chars\" — was checked and is **correct**, because the 50k→2M raise was a different setting. Every default published came from `Settings.model_fields`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **211 tests across the seven doc-parsing guards pass.** No guard weakened or edited; none needed updating, because sections and anchors were **added** rather than renamed.\n- `docs/ANTHROPIC-SUBSCRIPTION.md` untouched; its policy guard passes.\n- `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` verified byte-identical to each other.\n- Full suite: 4,989 passed / 8 failed, all `tests/scripts/test_install_ps1_*` — the standing Windows baseline.\n\n**No version bump:** markdown only, no production path touched.\n\n## Deliberately not done\n\n- `docs/CLAUDE-CODE-CONFIG.md` documents *Claude Code's own* env vars, not MCC's. Nothing in these releases touches it; it was not audited for upstream accuracy, which is a different job.\n- No restructuring. A reader who knows the old README still recognises it.\n\n## Residual risk\n\nThe Token Optimizer gets a full treatment in **both** README and USAGE, so the README is ~60 lines longer. That was deliberate — the trimming warning is the kind of thing a reader must not be able to miss — but if the README should stay thinner, that section is the one to cut down to a pointer.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
